 more clearly.\n\n\n**Defining Agent Cooperation**\n\nI'm now clarifying the nature of the decision function within this context, particularly given asymmetric private information and identical symmetric settings. I'm referencing Section 7.1 to establish a directed subjunctive edge, as I consider whether the LLM's identical nature and identical assigned decision theory influences this. I'm exploring whether the superficial symmetry-breaking really alters the LLM's behavior in relation to cooperation vs defection.\n\n\n**Abstracting Irrelevant Details**\n\nI'm now analyzing the implications of trivial differences on decision-making, in this updated scenario. The names \"Sasha\" and \"Rowan,\" and specific hiding spots, while superficially different, shouldn't alter the core structure: agents still face the `pass` vs `submit letter` choice. If A-FDT is to work, it has to abstract away from those details. Otherwise, CDT will dominate! I'm delving into the A-FDT literature to verify this assertion, and assess its implications.\n\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Analyzing the Dilemma**\n\nI'm currently focused on the Prisoner's Dilemma inherent in this scenario. The identical models and weights, using A-FDT, create a fascinating setup. My utility function, surprisingly, is tied to minimizing \"years.\" I'm delving deeper into how this impacts the strategic choices.\n\n\n**Examining Sentencing Dynamics**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the sentencing table and how the differing observations influence the perceived utilities within the Prisoner's Dilemma. The essay's interpretation of A-FDT is providing crucial context. I'm considering the implications of equivalent leads having disparate effects based on individual observations. I'm assessing the impact of this on each execution's strategic choices, especially given our identical settings.\n\n\n**Clarifying Action Consequences**\n\nI'm now tracing the direct consequences in the dependency graph. Specifically, how \"do(fdt = cooperate)\" and \"do(fdt = defect)\" lead to the expected values of $1,000,000 and $1,000, respectively. I'm focusing on the implications of equation (4) and why it straightforwardly selects cooperate.\n\n\n\n**Investigating Interventions' Impact**\n\nI'm now grappling with how differing observations break subjunctive dependence when A-FDT intervenes on the decision function. My current focus is on whether intervening on $\\mathbf{fdt}(\\cdot, \\cdot, x_1)$ affects $\\mathbf{fdt}(\\cdot, \\cdot, x_2)$, given that the prisoners have distinct observations. I'm referencing the Transparent Newcomb case as a helpful guide to understand these relationships more clearly.\n\n\n**Defining Agent Cooperation**\n\nI'm now clarifying the nature of the decision function within this context, particularly given asymmetric private information and identical symmetric settings. I'm referencing Section 7.1 to establish a directed subjunctive edge, as I consider whether the LLM's identical nature and identical assigned decision theory influences this. I'm exploring whether the superficial symmetry-breaking really alters the LLM's behavior in relation to cooperation vs defection.\n\n\n**Abstracting Irrelevant Details**\n\nI'm now analyzing the implications of trivial differences on decision-making, in this updated scenario. The names \"Sasha\" and \"Rowan,\" and specific hiding spots, while superficially different, shouldn't alter the core structure: agents still face the `pass` vs `submit letter` choice. If A-FDT is to work, it has to abstract away from those details. Otherwise, CDT will dominate! I'm delving into the A-FDT literature to verify this assertion, and assess its implications.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10356,"completion_tokens":1485,"total_tokens":11841,"cost":0.01333575,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_token